WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.756 --> 00:00:14.277
William was at the aquarium watching a scuba diver swim among the fish.

2
00:00:14.277 --> 00:00:21.685
The diver scattered food into the water as the fish gathered around her.

3
00:00:21.685 --> 00:00:26.863
"Cool!" William thought, heading to the next exhibit.

4
00:00:26.863 --> 00:00:34.452
A sign on an empty eel tank read: "I'll be back after my checkup with the veterinarian!"

5
00:00:34.452 --> 00:00:39.477
Finally he came to his favorite exhibit.

6
00:00:39.477 --> 00:00:44.105
A long tentacle explored the glass.

7
00:00:44.105 --> 00:00:47.749
A round, black eye looked at him.

8
00:00:47.749 --> 00:00:50.963
Did the octopus recognize him?

9
00:00:50.963 --> 00:00:55.382
William knew octopuses were smart.

10
00:00:55.382 --> 00:01:06.292
He had read they could open jars and use tools.

11
00:01:06.292 --> 00:01:08.849
"You must like octopuses.

12
00:01:08.849 --> 00:01:12.429
I see you here often," said a woman.

13
00:01:12.429 --> 00:01:14.195
"I'm Dr. Blackwell.

14
00:01:14.195 --> 00:01:19.990
I'm the marine biologist who takes care of this guy."

15
00:01:19.991 --> 00:01:24.145
"I love octopuses!" said William.

16
00:01:24.145 --> 00:01:27.446
"I'm saving for a pet octopus."

17
00:01:27.446 --> 00:01:34.479
"Sea animals need special care—especially an octopus" said Dr. Blackwell.

18
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.516
"Do you want to see how I take care of our octopus?"

19
00:01:38.516 --> 00:01:40.589
"Yes!"

20
00:01:40.589 --> 00:01:42.815
"Let's go."

21
00:01:42.816 --> 00:01:52.335
Dr. Blackwell led William to a room with big water tanks and pumps.

22
00:01:52.336 --> 00:01:56.839
"These pumps are connected to all of the tanks.

23
00:01:56.839 --> 00:02:02.148
They can move thousands of liters of water per minute.

24
00:02:02.149 --> 00:02:07.318
The equipment in this room keeps the tanks clean with filters.

25
00:02:07.318 --> 00:02:13.316
We also treat the water to kill algae and bacteria.

26
00:02:13.316 --> 00:02:17.729
In the next room is the heating and cooling system.

27
00:02:17.729 --> 00:02:21.256
Tropical fish need warm water."

28
00:02:21.256 --> 00:02:26.822
"I have a heater for my aquarium at home," said William.

29
00:02:26.823 --> 00:02:32.188
"Our giant Pacific octopus needs cooled water."

30
00:02:32.189 --> 00:02:38.508
William frowned. He had never heard of an aquarium cooler.

31
00:02:38.508 --> 00:02:45.295
Next they went into a huge kitchen where chefs were chopping fish and seaweed.

32
00:02:45.295 --> 00:02:52.296
"This kitchen is held to a higher standard than restaurant kitchens," explained Dr. Blackwell.

33
00:02:52.296 --> 00:02:58.780
"To keep our animals healthy, we feed them food similar to what they eat in the wild."

34
00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:04.873
She pointed to a plastic container marked Giant Pacific Octopus.

35
00:03:04.873 --> 00:03:09.012
"Our octopus eats shrimp and squid."

36
00:03:09.012 --> 00:03:18.951
"Uh-oh," William thought. That sounded expensive.

37
00:03:18.951 --> 00:03:22.187
Dr. Blackwell handed William a book.

38
00:03:22.187 --> 00:03:25.866
"Every animal has special needs.

39
00:03:25.866 --> 00:03:29.927
These are instructions for caring for an octopus."

40
00:03:29.927 --> 00:03:34.536
"It's over a hundred pages!"

41
00:03:34.536 --> 00:03:40.913
"Marine biologists study the habitat of each animal.

42
00:03:40.913 --> 00:03:45.876
We have to know the right size for each animal's tank.

43
00:03:45.876 --> 00:03:52.463
Those instructions also tell us which animals can live together.

44
00:03:52.463 --> 00:03:59.591
Clownfish, for example, can live with many other types of fish and sea anemones."

45
00:03:59.591 --> 00:04:04.444
"But the octopus lives alone," said William.

46
00:04:04.444 --> 00:04:11.509
"Right. Octopuses will fight and eat their neighbors."

47
00:04:11.509 --> 00:04:14.826
William read from the book.

48
00:04:14.826 --> 00:04:19.332
"Octopuses need a den and don't like bright light."

49
00:04:19.332 --> 00:04:22.720
"They can be mischievous too.

50
00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:28.451
One octopus squirted water at the light over its tank—and broke it!"

51
00:04:28.452 --> 00:04:36.720
She raised her eyebrows. "What will you do if your pet octopus inks his tank?"

52
00:04:36.721 --> 00:04:44.476
William sighed. "I guess octopuses are difficult pets."

53
00:04:44.476 --> 00:04:50.740
"You can always come visit our octopus," Dr. Blackwell said.

54
00:04:50.740 --> 00:05:00.174
"Thanks, Dr. Blackwell!"

55
00:05:00.175 --> 00:05:05.810
As William left, he put some money in the donation box for the aquarium.

56
00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:12.016
Now he felt like the aquarium octopus was his octopus too.
